#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
#  Project:      dfe-infra
#  File:         scripts/dfe-sweep
#  Purpose:      One read-only sweep of the open findings and work items across the DFE suite.
#  Language:     Python
#
#  License:      BUSL-1.1
#  Copyright:    (c) 2026 HYPERI PTY LIMITED
#
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = ["scalo>=2.31.1"]
# ///
"""Sweep every repo the DFE suite consists of for every open finding and work item on GitHub.

    scripts/dfe-sweep                                       # text report
    scripts/dfe-sweep --format json > sweep.json
    scripts/dfe-sweep --members                             # membership only, one search per topic
    scripts/dfe-sweep --check-bridge                        # and prove the bridge is a subset
    scripts/dfe-sweep --format slack --report-url https://example.com/r
    scripts/dfe-sweep --format slack --report-file sweep-report.txt > sweep-slack.json
    scripts/dfe-sweep --format slack --slack-title "Daily summary" --slack-timezone Europe/Berlin
    scripts/dfe-sweep --exclude dfe-hyperdx --tools dependabot,code_scanning

The shebang runs it through ``uv run --script``, which installs scalo from the block above; on
a host whose env cannot take ``-S``, run ``uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep``. It runs from any
directory: settings, versions.yaml and suite.yaml are found from the script's own path.

MEMBERSHIP is derived and never listed here. The stack the ``stack`` setting points at in
versions.yaml (``latest``, falling back to ``current`` when ``latest`` is empty) names the repos
under its ``apps:`` and ``content:`` maps; a commented-out pin is not a member, so uncommenting
one is the whole change that brings a repo in. Every suite.yaml node is a member unless it is
marked ``default_in_pass: false``. suite.yaml's build-cycle in-edges, walked transitively from
the stack, add every repo it depends on; the infra repo is always in; every unarchived repo in
the org carrying one of the ``member_topics`` is in, so tagging a repo is the whole change that
brings it in; then the ``include`` and ``exclude`` settings apply. Each member is printed with
every reason it is in.

SEVERITY. Only a severity a security tool assigned is ever critical: a Dependabot or repository
advisory's, or a code-scanning rule's security severity. A code-scanning rule without one takes
its SARIF level (error high, warning medium, note low). A secret alert carries no severity, so it
is high (medium once GitHub calls the secret inactive) and is counted on its own. A failed run
of a main workflow is high, a cancelled one low; CI annotations are medium (failure) or low
(warning); Renovate dashboard entries are medium (errored, repository problems) or low.

FINDINGS, read per member at the head of its default branch through ``gh api`` as GET only and
ranked by severity: open Dependabot, code-scanning and secret-scanning alerts (the secret value
is never requested), repository security advisories in triage or draft, open bot PRs, the
Renovate Dependency Dashboard's errored, rate-limited, pending and problem entries, and each
workflow's latest completed run on the default branch with its warning and failure annotations.
The workflows are listed once per repo and each active one's newest run is read from that
workflow's own runs list, one call each; a disabled workflow is skipped. A tool GitHub refuses
(403, 404), or a gh call that runs past ``gh_timeout_seconds``, is listed as not readable for
that repo, never as zero; a workflow whose runs cannot be read is named in the reason.

WORK ITEMS, listed and summarised per member but never severity-ranked: the active issues, the
scheduled issues, the issues to triage and the backlog. The backlog follows hyperi-ai's
``pm backlog`` model: the org project titled exactly as the repo, read with one GraphQL query
joining its Status to the Priority and Effort issue fields, grouped by Status, highest priority
first. A repo with no project is "no project"; a token that cannot list projects (it needs
``read:project``) is unreadable. An open issue is on the backlog when it carries the
``backlog.label`` label or sits at the backlog Status, the first of ``backlog.statuses``. The
label wins: a labelled issue is backlog whatever its project Status, and the release-blocker
label keeps an issue off the backlog whatever its Status. Every other open issue is sorted by
its labels (GATING below), so one at Next or In progress without the label is not on the
backlog. The backlog lists every project item at the backlog Status or carrying the label, and
each labelled issue the project does not hold. Label drift is a real conflict and nothing less:
an item at the backlog Status without the label, or a labelled item the project holds at
another Status. A labelled issue the project does not hold, a repo with no project and an item
with no Status set have nothing to contradict the label and are not drift.

GATING. Findings and active issues gate a release. The backlog, the scheduled issues, the issues
to triage and label drift never do. An open issue off the backlog is sorted by the org issue
label standard, whose gate labels hyperi-ci's manifest names. The command in ``labels.command``
prints that manifest and runs once per sweep, and its ``gate`` list must name ``labels.blocker``:

- carrying the blocker label (release-blocker): active, so it gates.
- carrying any other gate label (next-patch, next-minor): scheduled, so it does not gate.
- carrying no gate label: active when the issue's ``author_association`` is OWNER, MEMBER or
  COLLABORATOR, or its author is a bot (a login in ``bot_logins``, or a user of type Bot), and
  otherwise on the triage list, which does not gate.

The backlog label is a gate label too, and an issue carrying it is on the backlog before any of
this applies. The needs-decision label is a waiting label and changes none of it. A tool that
could not be read gates as well, since zero cannot be claimed over a read that did not happen,
except the backlog: its read only ever moves issues out of the gating count, so losing it
over-counts what gates and never hides any of it. A manifest that could not be read is reported
as the issues tool not readable on every member, which gates, and every issue off the backlog
counts as active meanwhile, which can only over-count.

``--check-bridge`` also reads the org's open Dependabot, code-scanning and secret-scanning
alerts, the steady state of what the org webhook alert bridge forwards, and fails if one on a
member is missing from the sweep. Alerts on repos that are not members are out of scope and
not reported.

SLACK. The sweep never posts to Slack and never reads a Slack token: ``--format slack`` prints
one JSON object to stdout for a separate poster, which owns the channel and the credential.

    {"summary": "<mrkdwn>", "detail": [{"group": "<name>", "text": "<mrkdwn>"}, ...],
     "stats": {"gating": 0, "findings": 0, "secrets": 0, "critical": 0, "high": 0,
               "medium": 0, "low": 0, "ci_failing": 0, "issues": 0, "backlog": 0,
               "bot_prs": 0, "drift": 0, "unreadable": 0, "members": 0},
     "keys": [{"key": "<stable id>", "group": "<name>", "line": "<mrkdwn>", "gates": true},
              ...]}

The poster posts ``summary`` as the parent message and each ``detail`` entry, in order, as a
thread reply. ``summary`` is at most 8 lines: the headline, the gating line, then secret
scanning, critical and high security findings, failing main CI, the medium and low counts, what
was not readable and the bridge check, each only when there is something to say. ``detail``
holds one reply or more per non-empty group (secret scanning, code scanning, Dependabot,
security advisories, CI on main, bot PRs and Renovate, active issues, not readable, issues to
triage, scheduled issues, backlog items, backlog drift), each under 3,500 characters, so every
group that gates is posted before the ones that never do. A group that runs over continues
under a ``(cont.)`` header for at most ``slack.group_replies`` replies (2 by default), so a long
group cannot crowd a later one out, and its last reply says how many of its lines were left out,
pointing at ``--report-url`` when one is given; the thread is capped at 20 replies the same way.
Every string that came from GitHub is escaped, so no title can mention a channel or inject a
link.

HEADLINE. ``summary`` opens ``*<title>*: stack <stack>, <n> members, <time>``. The title is the
``slack.title`` setting (``--slack-title``), "DFE suite sweep" by default. The time is when the
report was generated, in the ``slack.timezone`` setting's IANA zone (``--slack-timezone``, UTC by
default), to the minute and followed by the zone's own abbreviation for that instant:
``DFE suite sweep: stack 2.2.0, 22 members, 2026-10-13 05:00 UTC``. A zone with daylight saving
changes its abbreviation with the date, so Europe/Berlin reads CET in winter and CEST in summer.
A zone the host's tz database does not know is a run error naming the setting, for every format.

The gating line follows it, the gating count first and what does not gate after it:
``*Gating: 14* -- 11 findings, 3 active issues. Not gating: backlog 5 (P0 1, unprioritised 4),
2 scheduled, 1 to triage, 2 label drift``. A not-readable count is added on whichever side its
tools fall. A sweep with nothing open at all says ``All clear: nothing open on any member.``
instead.

AGES. Each item line in ``detail`` ends with how long ago its row was raised, counted from the
row's ``created_at`` to the report's generation time: ``(<1h)`` under an hour, ``(5h)`` under a
day, then whole days, floored, as ``(6d)``. A line that is clipped is cut before its age, never
through it. A not-readable line has no age. A backlog drift line takes the age of its issue.

``stats`` always carries every key above as an integer, counted from the same findings as the
summary's lines. ``gating`` is ``findings`` plus ``issues`` plus each not-readable tool other
than the backlog. ``findings`` counts every finding, ``issues`` the active issues and
``backlog`` the backlog. critical and high count code scanning, Dependabot and repository
advisories only, the numbers on the Security line. A secret alert counts under ``secrets`` and a
failed main run under ``ci_failing`` instead. medium and low count every tool, as the Medium
line does. ``--format json`` carries the same split as ``totals.gating``: ``total``,
``findings``, ``issues`` and ``unreadable``.

KEYS. ``keys`` is for a poster that posts only what is new: it diffs each run's keys against
the set it last posted, and it stores hashes of the keys, never the lines. There is one entry
per finding and work item in every group, in posting order, including a row the reply budget
cut from ``detail``. ``group`` is the group's name in ``detail``, and ``line`` is the row's line
exactly as ``detail`` renders it, escaped, clipped and ending in its age, so it changes as the
row ages while its key does not. ``gates`` is true for a row that counts toward ``gating``:
every finding, every active issue and each not-readable tool but the backlog. It is false for a
scheduled issue, an issue to triage, a backlog item, label drift and a backlog that was not
readable, so the gating count is zero exactly when no entry has ``gates`` true. A key appears
once per output, for the first row that has it, and stays the same from run to run while the
finding does:

- an alert, a repository advisory, an active issue or a bot PR: its html_url.
- a scheduled issue ``<repo>:scheduled:<issue url>``, an issue to triage
  ``<repo>:triage:<issue url>``, a backlog item ``<repo>:backlog:<issue url>`` and label drift
  ``<repo>:drift:<issue url>``, so an issue is keyed afresh when its labels move it between
  groups, and a backlog issue that also drifts is keyed in each group it is listed in.
- a failed main run ``<repo>:<workflow file>::<hash>`` of its conclusion, and a CI annotation
  ``<repo>:<workflow file>:<jobs>:<hash>`` of its level, file, title and message. Never the run
  or job URL, which changes every run.
- a Renovate dashboard entry ``<repo>:renovate_dashboard:<hash>`` of its section and text.
- a tool that was not readable: ``<repo>:<tool>``.

A hash is the first 12 hex digits of a SHA-256 of normalised text. CI text loses temp paths,
UUIDs, SHAs, runner names, durations and every other number, run ids and timestamps included,
but keeps an advisory id such as CVE-2026-1234 whole. Dashboard text loses temp paths, UUIDs
and SHAs only, so its version numbers still tell two updates apart.

REPORT FILE. ``--report-file PATH`` (setting ``report_file``, empty for none) also writes the
full text report to PATH, exactly what ``--format text`` prints, whatever ``--format`` sends to
stdout. A relative PATH is from the working directory. One run then yields the Slack JSON and
the report behind it, so a scheduled workflow can publish that report on the run
``--report-url`` links to:

    run_url="$GITHUB_SERVER_URL/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/actions/runs/$GITHUB_RUN_ID"
    scripts/dfe-sweep --format slack --report-url "$run_url" \\
        --report-file sweep-report.txt > sweep-slack.json
    { echo '```text'; cat sweep-report.txt; echo '```'; } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

A later step uploads ``sweep-report.txt`` with actions/upload-artifact, pinned by SHA.

CONFIGURATION is scalo's cascade (scalo.config), with the committed defaults in
config/defaults.yaml under ``sweep:``, which scalo reads as its defaults.yaml layer: the
settings load with the repository root as the working directory, wherever the script was run
from. settings.yaml and settings.<env>.yaml (in the repository root, its config/, or
~/.config/dfe-sweep/), a .env, then ``DFE_SWEEP__<KEY>`` environment variables override it, and
the flags below override those. Examples: ``DFE_SWEEP__EXCLUDE='["dfe-hyperdx"]'``,
``DFE_SWEEP__TOOLS__ISSUES=false``, ``DFE_SWEEP__SLACK__GROUP_REPLIES=3``,
``DFE_SWEEP__SLACK__TIMEZONE=Europe/Berlin``.

gh is the only credential: the caller's own gh login or GH_TOKEN. scalo's .env layer exports
every key of the .env it finds into the process environment, so the caller's environment is put
back once the settings are read, before gh or anything else runs.

Exit codes: 0 the sweep ran (findings are data, not failure), 1 a run error, 2 bad arguments,
3 ``--check-bridge`` found an alert on a member that the sweep did not.
"""

import sys
from pathlib import Path

SCRIPTS = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
REPO_ROOT = SCRIPTS.parent
if str(SCRIPTS) not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, str(SCRIPTS))

import argparse
import functools
import io
import json
import os
from collections.abc import Callable, Iterator
from contextlib import contextmanager
from dataclasses import dataclass
from datetime import UTC, tzinfo
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo, ZoneInfoNotFoundError

from dfe_suite import sweep
from dfe_suite.graph import load_graph
from dfe_suite.proc import FleetError

TAG = "dfe-sweep"
APP_NAME = "dfe-sweep"
ENV_PREFIX = "DFE"
SECTION = "sweep"
FORMATS = ("text", "json", "slack")

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_ERROR = 1
EXIT_BRIDGE_GAP = 3

RATE_LIMIT_JQ = "{remaining: .resources.core.remaining, limit: .resources.core.limit} | tojson"


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Options:
    """The settings after the command line is laid over the cascade.

    Attributes:
        org: Owner for unqualified names and for the org-wide alert lists.
        infra: The infra repo's suite name.
        versions_file: The versions.yaml membership is read from.
        suite_dir: The checkout whose suite.yaml dfe-stack reads.
        stack: The stack pointer.
        topics: Repo topics whose every unarchived repo in the org is a member.
        include: Names or slugs added to the derived membership.
        exclude: Names or slugs removed from it.
        derive_suite: False takes members only from infra, topics and include.
        output: ``text``, ``json`` or ``slack``.
        project_limit: Most org projects listed when finding each repo's backlog project.
        labels_command: The argv that prints hyperi-ci's label manifest as JSON.
        labels_blocker: The gate label that holds a release cut.
        labels_timeout: Seconds the manifest command may take.
        report_url: Where the full report is published, for the Slack cut-off line, or empty.
        report_file: Where the text report is also written, or None for nowhere.
        group_replies: The most Slack thread replies one group may take.
        slack_title: The name the Slack headline opens with.
        slack_zone: The zone the Slack headline's time is shown in.
        gh_timeout: Seconds one gh call may take before its read is reported unreadable.
        config: The collection settings.
    """

    org: str
    infra: str
    versions_file: Path
    suite_dir: Path
    stack: str
    topics: tuple[str, ...]
    include: tuple[str, ...]
    exclude: tuple[str, ...]
    derive_suite: bool
    output: str
    project_limit: int
    labels_command: tuple[str, ...]
    labels_blocker: str
    labels_timeout: int
    group_replies: int
    slack_title: str
    slack_zone: tzinfo
    gh_timeout: int
    report_url: str
    report_file: Path | None
    config: sweep.SweepConfig


def _fail(key: str, value: object, wanted: str) -> FleetError:
    return FleetError(f"setting {SECTION}.{key} is {value!r}; it must be {wanted}")


def _text(settings: dict, key: str, name: str | None = None) -> str:
    value = settings.get(key)
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
        raise _fail(name or key, value, "a non-empty string")
    return value.strip()


def _names(value: object, key: str) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """A list setting; an env var or a repeated flag may also give comma-separated strings."""
    if value is None:
        return ()
    if isinstance(value, str):
        value = [value]
    if not isinstance(value, list | tuple):
        raise _fail(key, value, "a list")
    return tuple(sweep.split_names(value))


def _argv(value: object, key: str) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """A command setting: a non-empty list of non-empty strings."""
    wanted = "a non-empty list of non-empty strings"
    if not isinstance(value, list | tuple) or not value:
        raise _fail(key, value, wanted)
    if not all(isinstance(part, str) and part.strip() for part in value):
        raise _fail(key, value, wanted)
    return tuple(value)


def _count(value: object, key: str) -> int:
    """A whole-number setting of at least 1."""
    wanted = "a whole number of at least 1"
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int | str):
        raise _fail(key, value, wanted)
    try:
        number = int(value)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise _fail(key, value, wanted) from exc
    if number < 1:
        raise _fail(key, value, wanted)
    return number


def _flag(value: object, key: str) -> bool:
    """A true/false setting, refused when it is anything else."""
    if not isinstance(value, bool):
        raise _fail(key, value, "true or false")
    return value


def _path(value: str) -> Path:
    path = Path(value).expanduser()
    return path if path.is_absolute() else REPO_ROOT / path


def _tools(value: object, cli: str | None) -> frozenset[str]:
    """The enabled tools: the --tools list when given, else each one the settings switch on."""
    if cli is not None:
        chosen = set(_names(cli, "tools"))
    elif isinstance(value, dict):
        chosen = set()
        for tool, on in value.items():
            if not isinstance(on, bool):
                raise _fail(f"tools.{tool}", on, "true or false")
            if on:
                chosen.add(tool)
        unknown = set(value) - set(sweep.TOOLS)
        if unknown:
            raise _fail("tools", sorted(unknown), f"keys from {', '.join(sweep.TOOLS)}")
    else:
        raise _fail("tools", value, "a map of tool to true or false")
    unknown = chosen - set(sweep.TOOLS)
    if unknown:
        raise FleetError(f"unknown tool(s) {', '.join(sorted(unknown))}; "
                         f"known: {', '.join(sweep.TOOLS)}")
    if not chosen:
        raise FleetError("no tools are enabled, so there is nothing to sweep")
    return frozenset(chosen)


def effective(settings: dict, args: argparse.Namespace) -> Options:
    """Lay the command line over the cascade's ``sweep`` settings and check every value.

    Args:
        settings: The resolved ``sweep:`` section, as plain data.
        args: The parsed command line; a flag left unset defers to the settings.

    Returns:
        The options a run uses.

    Raises:
        FleetError: On a missing or malformed setting, naming the key.
    """

    def pick(flag: object, key: str) -> object:
        return flag if flag is not None else settings.get(key)

    output = str(pick(args.format, "format") or "").strip()
    if output not in FORMATS:
        raise _fail("format", output, f"one of {', '.join(FORMATS)}")
    picked = {
        "org": pick(args.org, "org"),
        "stack": pick(args.stack, "stack"),
        "versions_file": pick(args.versions_file, "versions_file"),
        "suite_dir": pick(args.suite_dir, "suite_dir"),
        "infra_repo": settings.get("infra_repo"),
    }
    org = _text(picked, "org")
    bots = _names(settings.get("bot_logins"), "bot_logins")
    if not bots:
        raise _fail("bot_logins", settings.get("bot_logins"), "a non-empty list of logins")
    backlog = _section(settings, "backlog")
    labels = _section(settings, "labels")
    slack = _section(settings, "slack")
    statuses = _names(backlog.get("statuses"), "backlog.statuses")
    if not statuses:
        raise _fail("backlog.statuses", backlog.get("statuses"), "a non-empty list")
    config = sweep.SweepConfig(
        tools=_tools(settings.get("tools"), args.tools),
        bot_logins=frozenset(bots),
        workers=_count(pick(args.workers, "workers"), "workers"),
        org=org,
        backlog=sweep.BacklogModel(
            label=_text(backlog, "label", "backlog.label"),
            statuses=statuses,
            priorities=_names(backlog.get("priorities"), "backlog.priorities"),
        ),
    )
    if args.check_bridge and not set(sweep.BRIDGED) <= config.tools:
        raise FleetError(
            f"--check-bridge compares against {', '.join(sweep.BRIDGED)}, so all three must "
            f"be enabled"
        )
    return Options(
        org=org,
        infra=_text(picked, "infra_repo"),
        versions_file=_path(_text(picked, "versions_file")),
        suite_dir=_path(_text(picked, "suite_dir")),
        stack=_text(picked, "stack"),
        topics=_names(settings.get("member_topics"), "member_topics"),
        include=_names(args.include if args.include is not None else settings.get("include"),
                       "include"),
        exclude=_names(args.exclude if args.exclude is not None else settings.get("exclude"),
                       "exclude"),
        derive_suite=_flag(settings.get("derive_suite", True), "derive_suite"),
        output=output,
        project_limit=_count(backlog.get("project_limit"), "backlog.project_limit"),
        labels_command=_argv(labels.get("command"), "labels.command"),
        labels_blocker=_text(labels, "blocker", "labels.blocker"),
        labels_timeout=_count(labels.get("timeout_seconds"), "labels.timeout_seconds"),
        report_url=_report_url(pick(args.report_url, "report_url")),
        report_file=_report_file(pick(args.report_file, "report_file")),
        group_replies=_count(slack.get("group_replies"), "slack.group_replies"),
        slack_title=_slack_title(
            args.slack_title if args.slack_title is not None else slack.get("title")
        ),
        slack_zone=_slack_zone(
            args.slack_timezone if args.slack_timezone is not None else slack.get("timezone")
        ),
        gh_timeout=_count(settings.get("gh_timeout_seconds"), "gh_timeout_seconds"),
        config=config,
    )


def _section(settings: dict, key: str) -> dict:
    """A nested settings map, or a run error naming it."""
    value = settings.get(key)
    if not isinstance(value, dict):
        raise _fail(key, value, "a map")
    return value


def _report_url(value: object) -> str:
    """An https URL for the Slack cut-off line, or empty for none."""
    url = str(value or "").strip()
    if url and not sweep.safe_url(url):
        raise _fail("report_url", value, "an https URL with no spaces, <, > or |")
    return url


def _slack_title(value: object) -> str:
    """The name the Slack headline opens with, which must be a non-empty string."""
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
        raise _fail("slack.title", value, "a non-empty string")
    return value.strip()


def _slack_zone(value: object) -> tzinfo:
    """The IANA zone the Slack headline's time is shown in, or a run error naming the setting.

    UTC is answered without the host's tz database, so the default needs none.
    """
    name = value.strip() if isinstance(value, str) else ""
    if name == "UTC":
        return UTC
    try:
        return ZoneInfo(name)
    except (ZoneInfoNotFoundError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise _fail(
            "slack.timezone", value, "an IANA zone name such as UTC or Europe/Berlin"
        ) from exc


def _report_file(value: object) -> Path | None:
    """The file the text report is also written to, from the working directory, or None."""
    if value is not None and not isinstance(value, str):
        raise _fail("report_file", value, "a file path, or empty for none")
    text = (value or "").strip()
    if not text:
        return None
    # Checked before the sweep runs, so a bad path fails in a second rather than after it.
    path = Path(text).expanduser().absolute()
    if path.is_dir() or not path.parent.is_dir():
        raise _fail("report_file", value, "a file path in a directory that exists")
    return path


def _write_report(path: Path | None, text: str) -> None:
    """Write the text report to ``path`` when one is set, ending in a newline as stdout does."""
    if path is None:
        return
    try:
        path.write_text(text + "\n", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n")
    except OSError as exc:
        raise FleetError(f"cannot write the report file {path}: {exc}") from exc


@contextmanager
def caller_state_kept(workdir: Path) -> Iterator[None]:
    """Run the block in ``workdir``, then put the caller's directory and environment back.

    scalo's .env layer exports every key of the .env it finds, so without this a token in
    that file would become gh's identity for the rest of the run.
    """
    caller_env = dict(os.environ)
    caller_cwd = os.getcwd()
    os.chdir(workdir)
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        os.chdir(caller_cwd)
        os.environ.clear()
        os.environ.update(caller_env)


def load_settings() -> tuple[dict, Callable[[str], None], Callable[[str], None]]:
    """Read the ``sweep`` section through scalo's cascade and start scalo's logger.

    Returns:
        The section as plain data, and the logger's info and warning methods.

    Raises:
        FleetError: If scalo is not installed, or no ``sweep`` section resolves.
    """
    # scalo finds its files from the working directory when it is imported, so it is imported
    # and read from the repository root, wherever the script was started.
    with caller_state_kept(REPO_ROOT):
        # Names the scalo app, so its user layer is ~/.config/dfe-sweep/, without scalo
        # importing every installed distribution to guess the name.
        os.environ.setdefault("APP_NAME", APP_NAME)
        try:
            from scalo.config import get_config
            from scalo.logger import logger, setup
        except ImportError as exc:
            raise FleetError(
                "scalo is not installed here; run `uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep`"
            ) from exc
        section = get_config(env_prefix=ENV_PREFIX).get(SECTION)
        settings = dict(section) if isinstance(section, dict) else section
        setup(level="INFO", log_format="text", use_emojis=False, otel_tracing=False)
    if not isinstance(settings, dict) or not settings:
        raise FleetError(
            f"no `{SECTION}:` settings resolved from {REPO_ROOT / 'config' / 'defaults.yaml'}"
        )
    return settings, logger.info, logger.warning


def _members_report(stack: str, members: list[sweep.Member], output: str) -> str:
    """The membership alone: text, or JSON for both machine formats."""
    rows = [{"name": m.name, "repo": m.repo, "why": list(m.why)} for m in members]
    if output in ("json", "slack"):
        return json.dumps({"stack": stack, "members": rows}, indent=2)
    return sweep.render_members(stack, rows)


type Loader = Callable[[], tuple[dict, Callable[[str], None], Callable[[str], None]]]


def run(args: argparse.Namespace, load: Loader = load_settings) -> int:
    """One sweep, end to end. Returns the exit code.

    Args:
        args: The parsed command line.
        load: Where the settings and the progress log come from; scalo's cascade in a run.
    """
    settings, info, warning = load()
    options = effective(settings, args)
    root = sweep.read_versions(options.versions_file)
    graph = load_graph(dfe_infra=options.suite_dir)
    api = functools.partial(sweep.gh_api, timeout=options.gh_timeout)
    stack, members, notes = sweep.resolve_members(
        root,
        graph,
        org=options.org,
        pointer=options.stack,
        infra=options.infra,
        topics=sweep.topic_members(options.org, options.topics, api),
        include=options.include,
        exclude=options.exclude,
        derive_suite=options.derive_suite,
    )
    for note in notes:
        warning(note)
    if args.members:
        _write_report(options.report_file, _members_report(stack, members, "text"))
        print(_members_report(stack, members, options.output), flush=True)
        return EXIT_OK

    graphql = functools.partial(sweep.gh_graphql, timeout=options.gh_timeout)
    try:
        (limit,) = api("rate_limit", RATE_LIMIT_JQ, False)
    except (sweep.GhError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise FleetError(f"gh cannot read the GitHub API: {exc}") from exc
    info(
        f"stack {stack}: {len(members)} member(s), tools {', '.join(sorted(options.config.tools))}"
        f", API calls left {limit.get('remaining')}/{limit.get('limit')}"
    )
    projects = None
    if "backlog" in options.config.tools:
        projects = sweep.project_index(
            options.org, options.project_limit, timeout=options.gh_timeout
        )
        if projects.error:
            warning(f"backlog projects not readable, so every backlog is: {projects.error}")
        elif len(projects.numbers) >= options.project_limit:
            warning(
                f"listed {len(projects.numbers)} projects, the backlog.project_limit; a repo "
                f"whose project is past it reads as no project"
            )
    labels = None
    if "issues" in options.config.tools:
        labels = sweep.read_issue_labels(
            options.labels_command, options.labels_blocker, timeout=options.labels_timeout
        )
        if labels.error:
            warning(
                f"issue label standard not readable, so every issue off the backlog gates: "
                f"{labels.error}"
            )
    results = sweep.sweep(
        members,
        api,
        options.config,
        progress=info,
        graphql=graphql,
        projects=projects,
        labels=labels,
    )

    bridge = None
    gap = False
    if args.check_bridge:
        try:
            alerts = sweep.bridge_alerts(options.org, api)
        except sweep.GhError as exc:
            raise FleetError(f"cannot read the org alert lists for --check-bridge: {exc}") from exc
        missing, outside = sweep.bridge_gap(alerts, results)
        bridge = sweep.bridge_section(missing, len(alerts) - len(outside))
        gap = bool(missing)

    report = sweep.build_report(stack, results, options.config.tools, bridge)
    text = sweep.render_text(report, options.config.backlog)
    # Written before stdout, so a run that cannot write it prints nothing a poster would post.
    _write_report(options.report_file, text)
    if options.output == "json":
        print(json.dumps(report, indent=2), flush=True)
    elif options.output == "slack":
        slack = sweep.render_slack(
            report,
            options.config.backlog,
            options.report_url,
            options.group_replies,
            options.slack_title,
            options.slack_zone,
        )
        print(json.dumps(slack, indent=2), flush=True)
    else:
        print(text, flush=True)
    return EXIT_BRIDGE_GAP if gap else EXIT_OK


def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    """Assemble the argument parser. An unset flag defers to the config cascade."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=TAG,
        description=(
            "Collect every open GitHub-side finding on every repo the DFE suite consists of, "
            "ranked by severity, and summarise each repo's active issues and backlog. Read-only."
        ),
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        epilog=(
            "examples:\n"
            "  uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep\n"
            "  uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep --members\n"
            "  uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep --format json --check-bridge\n"
            "  uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep --format slack --report-file report.txt\n"
            "  uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep --include vector-vrl --exclude dfe-hyperdx\n"
            "  env DFE_SWEEP__TOOLS__ISSUES=false uv run --script scripts/dfe-sweep\n"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument("--format", choices=FORMATS, help="report format (setting: format)")
    parser.add_argument("--org", help="owner for unqualified names and the org alert lists")
    parser.add_argument("--stack", help="latest, current, or a stack key in versions.yaml")
    parser.add_argument("--versions-file", help="versions.yaml to read membership from")
    parser.add_argument("--suite-dir", help="checkout holding suite.yaml and scripts/dfe-stack")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--include",
        action="append",
        metavar="NAME",
        help="add a repo, by suite name or owner/name; repeat or comma-separate",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--exclude",
        action="append",
        metavar="NAME",
        help="drop a repo, by suite name or owner/name; repeat or comma-separate",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--tools",
        metavar="LIST",
        help=f"comma-separated tools to run, instead of the settings; from {', '.join(sweep.TOOLS)}",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--workers", type=int, help="repos read at once")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--report-url",
        help="where the full report is published, named by the Slack cut-off line",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--report-file",
        metavar="PATH",
        help="also write the text report here, whatever --format prints (setting: report_file)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--slack-title",
        metavar="TEXT",
        help="the name the Slack headline opens with (setting: slack.title)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--slack-timezone",
        metavar="ZONE",
        help="IANA zone the Slack headline's time is shown in (setting: slack.timezone)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--members",
        action="store_true",
        help="print the membership and stop; reads only the member topics' repo lists",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--check-bridge",
        action="store_true",
        help="fail when an open org alert on a member is missing from the sweep",
    )
    return parser


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None, load: Loader = load_settings) -> int:
    """Entry point. Returns the process exit code.

    Args:
        argv: The arguments, or None for the process's own.
        load: Where the settings and the progress log come from; scalo's cascade in a run.
    """
    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):
        if isinstance(stream, io.TextIOWrapper):
            stream.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    try:
        return run(args, load)
    except FleetError as exc:
        print(f"[{TAG}] ERROR: {exc}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        return EXIT_ERROR
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print(f"[{TAG}] interrupted", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        return 130


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
